          "type": "turn",
          "question": "At what blood pressure reading is hypertension diagnosed, and do the major bodies agree?",
          "answer": "Hypertension is diagnosed when blood pressure readings are persistently at least 130/80 mm Hg, according to guidelines from major health organizations in the United States (source: Major cardiology society consensus guidance, \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets\"). However, the definition of hypertension varies internationally. \n\nIn the United States, Stage 1 hypertension starts at 130-139 mm Hg systolic or 80-89 mm Hg diastolic. Conversely, European guidelines define hypertension as beginning at 140/90 mm Hg, categorizing the 130-139/80-89 range as \"high-normal blood pressure\" rather than as Stage 1 hypertension (source: \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets\").\n\nThus, there is a disagreement between U.S. and European guidelines regarding the threshold for diagnosing hypertension. Each organization uses slightly different criteria, leading to variations in how many individuals are classified as hypertensive.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
